  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nRidging continues to move eastward with southwest flow filling in \nfrom the west. Warm air and moisture is advecting into the 925mb to \n700mb layer, thus sustaining a low cloud deck and occasional mist \nand light rain. Visibility could be impacted this evening and \novernight, with a chance for 1 to 3 mile visibility during peak \ntrick or treat hours due to mist and/or patchy fog. Warm \ntemperatures are expected Sunday into Monday, with afternoon highs \npossibly approaching the 60s. This will be dependent on cloud cover, \nwhich could be very slow to clear Sunday morning.\n\n...Rain Event Monday/Tuesday...\n\nA slow moving H5 trough is expected to deepen as we head into Sunday \nand Monday. This will bring widespread rain chances into the area \nthrough Tuesday, with around an 80 percent chance of at least a half \ninch of rain across southeast North Dakota and west central \nMinnesota. Chances decrease further north and west. The heaviest \nrain will occur in response to a deformation zone that forms as the \nupper low moves off to the northeast. As such, the track of the low \nwill ultimately determine where the heaviest amounts fall, which \ncould be upwards of 2 inches to 3 inches inside the deformation \nband. Outside this band, the range will be lower, likely in the 0.5 \ninch to 1 inch range.",
